<v Joe>Like did the fall?

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:45.300
<v Joe>Was that supposed to like signify something or like give us a cliffhanger?

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:47.360
<v Joe>He got a CT. Made him fall.

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:48.820
<v Mike>He got a concussion.

00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:51.480
<v Joe>Like why did he fall and why is he all upset?

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:52.620
<v Joe>And what's he looking at?

00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:53.960
<v Joe>I am actually kind of.

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:56.380
<v Joe>Now I'm realizing I'm kind of sucked in by this cliffhanger.

00:28:56.760 --> 00:28:58.120
<v Mike>Here's why I'd be upset.

00:28:59.420 --> 00:29:06.180
<v Sean>I've made my way down to this ancient city.

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:07.640
<v Sean>There is water.

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:10.260
<v Sean>Yes. But I don't see food anywhere.

00:29:10.260 --> 00:29:11.900
<v Sean>I don't see other people.

00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:17.320
<v Sean>I know that I'm probably going to die in the next couple of weeks down here.

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:30.580
<v Sean>So maybe the to be continued is like you kind of play a game about him starving to death over the course of however many days or weeks.

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:32.460
<v Joe>You have to dig your own grave.

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:34.440
<v Sean>Yeah. That's probably part of it.

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:37.300
<v Sean>Yeah. So that's how I see it.

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:40.560
<v Joe>Oh. Speaking of like graves and deaths.

00:29:41.260 --> 00:29:44.580
<v Joe>I don't want to gloss over the disturbing death animation in this.

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:45.860
<v Mike>Oh where you just sort of melt?

00:29:46.160 --> 00:29:50.000
<v Joe>Yeah. You like melt into like a thin puddle of yourself.

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:51.800
<v Joe>But like your clothes are still there.

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:53.240
<v Sean>Yeah. Like very weird.

00:29:54.880 --> 00:29:55.780
<v Sean>I'm a fan.

00:29:56.940 --> 00:30:00.880
<v Sean>I think just to move more just into the animations in general.

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:05.260
<v Sean>I would say that this game may be over animated.

00:30:06.740 --> 00:30:12.120
<v Sean>And my only reason why is I don't know how many games we've played recently.

00:30:13.340 --> 00:30:23.740
<v Sean>Maybe maybe like three four years ago in the timeline this might have been more of like a thing that we're just like oh well they didn't realize how bad that felt yet.

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:30.120
<v Sean>But does it need a turnaround animation that you then need to press the button again to start moving forward again?

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:31.680
<v Sean>Do we need that?

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:34.940
<v Mike>Yes. Wasn't a fan.

00:30:35.780 --> 00:30:42.880
<v Joe>Yeah. No. It is a little like it's like too many frames that you know that are not like going fast enough.

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:45.020
<v Sean>You can tell I'm technically savvy.

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:54.380
<v Sean>Yeah. I do like the animation of sort of shimmying into a crawl space because that's very smooth and the game just kind of knows when you want to do that.

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:58.420
<v Sean>But yeah. Don't think it needed the turnaround animation.

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:07.160
<v Mike>All of the characters including Digger like are pretty well designed and have clear clean reads and animations and everything.

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:08.840
<v Mike>It's just kind of like the world around them.

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:13.940
<v Mike>Granted it's in a cave like I think it's supposed to read this way but it's just all kind of like blah otherwise.

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:18.940
<v Mike>Like the enemies and you are like the colorful part of the game.

00:31:18.940 --> 00:31:19.820
<v Mike>Yeah.

00:31:22.140 --> 00:31:28.860
<v Joe>And the way that you move too is it almost kind of reminds me of Mario where you've got a little bit of slidiness to you.

00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:30.280
<v Joe>There's a little weight. There's a little momentum.

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:35.360
<v Joe>Which we always praise about Mario but I don't know if it's what was needed here.

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.360
<v Joe>Because like you're digging.

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:40.520
<v Joe>You're kind of like taking it a little slower than Mario.

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:47.660
<v Joe>You're not doing these big giant platform jumps where you get running starts and you know jumping on enemies or anything like that.

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:51.200
<v Joe>Like I feel like you want to feel a little more like in control here.

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:55.180
<v Joe>And there's an ice level later that lets you slide and like that's an interesting like shake up.

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:57.940
<v Joe>But like we've already been kind of sliding throughout this whole game.

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:00.580
<v Joe>So it feels like a little I don't know.

00:32:00.700 --> 00:32:06.900
<v Joe>It just feels like unnecessary levels of physics there.

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:16.900
<v Sean>Yeah. I want to say that the physics and just the game feel, the controls are well designed but is it well designed for this game specifically?

00:32:17.200 --> 00:32:18.540
<v Sean>Right. Maybe not.

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:21.060
<v Sean>It does. They are smooth though.

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:25.140
<v Sean>It just doesn't actually, doesn't actually mesh.

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:35.680
<v Mike>In June 2018, a youth soccer team and their assistant coach became trapped in the Tam Luang cave system in northern Thailand after heavy rains flooded the passages.

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:45.220
<v Mike>The 12 boys aged 11 to 16 and their 25 year old coach had gone into the cave on June 23rd after practice, not realizing that the monsoon rain was coming.

00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:52.620
<v Mike>Rising water and strong currents cut them off from the entrance and left them with no contact with the outside world for more than a week.

00:32:52.620 --> 00:33:02.680
<v Mike>A huge international rescue effort formed, drawing in specialized divers, rescue workers, and officials from many countries as global attention focused on the cave.

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:04.400
<v Sean>And Elon Musk.

00:33:29.960 --> 00:33:32.320
<v Mike>And Elon Musk.

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:34.560
<v Mike>And all of them were successfully rescued.

00:33:34.560 --> 00:33:42.900
<v Mike>The mission involved up to 10,000 people and enormous resources, but it also cost the lives of two Thai Navy SEALs.

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:49.660
<v Mike>Hmm. You have to imagine that's what happened in the search for little Digger T Rock as well in this universe.

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:51.280
<v Sean>I was wondering where that was going.

00:33:51.520 --> 00:33:53.820
<v Joe>I thought this was the sequels and spinoff section.

00:33:54.780 --> 00:34:00.860
<v Mike>Right? They just, the Thailand cave thing all over again, but in this universe with Digger T Rock.

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:04.280
<v Sean>I don't think anyone's looking for this kid.

00:34:04.540 --> 00:34:13.260
<v Sean>I think that really the reason that he does this to himself is because there's no one that would look for him.

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:14.940
<v Mike>Yeah, that's why he goes down there.

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:17.880
<v Sean>Exactly. Like, you don't, there are no side characters.

00:34:18.200 --> 00:34:23.000
<v Sean>There's no, the only people that you meet want to kill you.

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:30.560
<v Sean>I think that this kid has a lot that he's not, like, he's probably pretty sad all the time.

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:32.840
<v Joe>Yeah, there's a lot to read between the lines in this.

00:34:32.920 --> 00:34:34.580
<v Joe>This is, like, a very well-written story.

00:34:34.740 --> 00:34:37.320
<v Joe>Yeah. It doesn't just, like, spoon-feed it to you either.

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:41.820
<v Joe>You really have to, like, you really have to see, like, you make your own interpretations off of it.

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:45.200
<v Joe>Yes. So, like, I think the story is one of the better stories we've had on the NES.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:52.900
<v Mike>All right. Well, then I guess it's just time to see if Digger T Rock can excavate a place on the Essential Games list.

00:34:56.580 --> 00:34:58.600
<v Mike>I did not have a great time with this game.

00:34:59.540 --> 00:35:19.620
<v Mike>I think my biggest problem was just how, not tedious, but how boring everything felt to get started in each level and, like, the necessary collecting of what feels like optional items in order to complete both these stages and later stages.

00:35:20.800 --> 00:35:25.140
<v Mike>It just didn't feel as cleverly designed as some other rare games that we're used to.

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:27.880
<v Mike>And so I'm going to put it as a no.

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:30.400
<v Mike>I don't dismiss that somebody could have fun with it.

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:32.760
<v Mike>I'm not saying it's a badly designed game.

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:34.580
<v Mike>It's just not something I had fun with.

00:35:34.820 --> 00:35:37.840
<v Sean>Sean? Yeah, I wasn't huge on it.

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:42.720
<v Sean>I do think this had to have been a pretty big influence on Spelunky, though.

00:35:43.760 --> 00:35:49.140
<v Sean>Just with, like, there's even ghosts that float around in a similar way.

00:35:49.440 --> 00:35:56.800
<v Sean>And, like, Spelunky feels a lot more, feels much better and is a lot more fun.

00:35:57.660 --> 00:36:00.940
<v Sean>But there's just so many borrowed, like, aspects that...

00:36:01.520 --> 00:36:03.880
<v Sean>So that means nothing to do with this game.

00:36:04.020 --> 00:36:05.540
<v Sean>This game is fine.

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:06.900
<v Sean>It's pretty fine.

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:09.500
<v Sean>It looks and feels fun.

00:36:10.820 --> 00:36:17.300
<v Sean>Your mileage may vary on how fun it actually is to play in terms of, like, that whole, like, execution thing.

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:23.360
<v Sean>But, yeah, it's otherwise pretty forgettable and not essential.

00:36:23.840 --> 00:36:27.720
<v Joe>Joe? I think I did have a little more fun with this.

00:36:28.180 --> 00:36:32.380
<v Joe>I think it's a very flawed but very charming game.

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:33.700
<v Joe>I think that's where I've landed on it.

00:36:33.780 --> 00:36:35.440
<v Joe>Like, I thought that...

00:36:35.440 --> 00:36:37.700
<v Joe>I was enjoying the different mechanics.

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:38.620
<v Joe>I was enjoying the movement.

00:36:38.620 --> 00:36:40.060
<v Joe>And even, like, the design.

00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:42.520
<v Joe>I know that the cave itself is kind of lackluster.

00:36:42.520 --> 00:36:50.560
<v Joe>But the enemies and you yourself are, like, pretty charming little chibi characters and stuff.

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:55.620
<v Joe>And I think it's maybe teeters on the edge of a play it for me.

00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:58.060
<v Joe>But also definitely not essential.

00:36:58.180 --> 00:37:02.620
<v Joe>Nothing to write home about. I think it's just another situation of, like, you know, we start one of these each week.

00:37:02.760 --> 00:37:08.140
<v Joe>I'm more often than not, we play a game that, like, eh, it's not very good.

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:11.140
<v Joe>So, like, when there is one like this, I'm like, oh, yeah, I enjoyed this.

00:37:11.140 --> 00:37:15.360
<v Joe>You know, it's just not some, like, crazy essential game to me.

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:16.320
<v Sean>So, definitely not.

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:19.180
<v Mike>Sean, you're forgiven for not remembering.

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:27.000
<v Mike>But we did play a game on this podcast called Spelunker back in September of 1987.

00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:32.320
<v Mike>And I feel like that was probably the major influence on Spelunky.

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:35.960
